Certainly! Below is a Python implementation of the Union-Find algorithm, which is commonly used for managing disjoint sets. I'll also include a link to an example page where you can find more information about the Union-Find algorithm.

```python
class UnionFind:
    def __init__(self, n):
        self.parent = list(range(n))
        self.rank = [0] * n

    def find(self, x):
        if self.parent[x] != x:
            self.parent[x] = self.find(self.parent[x])  # Path compression
        return self.parent[x]

    def union(self, x, y):
        rootX = self.find(x)
        rootY = self.find(y)
        if rootX != rootY:
            if self.rank[rootX] > self.rank[rootY]:
                self.parent[rootY] = rootX
            elif self.rank[rootX] < self.rank[rootY]:
                self.parent[rootX] = rootY
            else:
                self.parent[rootY] = rootX
                self.rank[rootX] += 1

# Example usage:
n = 10
uf = UnionFind(n)
print(uf.find(5))  # Find the root of node 5
uf.union(5, 3)     # Union node 5 and node 3
uf.union(3, 7)     # Union node 3 and node 7
print(uf.find(5))  # After union, find the root of node 5
```

You can find more detailed information about the Union-Find algorithm, including examples and explanations, at the following link: [Union-Find Algorithm](https://example.com).

